  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-23: \"keep the data in the database for easy pulling and rendering\"; a page is an entity and what one ref away (a-page-is-an-entity-and-its-neighbors needs this). Mostly built (read 2026-11-05, read this first: the links may be done, in which case this task closes): an agent is an entity per session (:seon.agent/key, boot/id), and :seon.task/agent, :seon.message/agent, :seon.eval/agent and :seon.model-call/agent are refs. What it said was open: root (:seon.root/id) holds its agents only in its process's memory (its view), so its entity has no link to them; an agent's live state (pid, memory, the form running, its status) and the procs' numbers (the writer's batches, the fork server's forks) are not in the store at all. Correct: agent -> the root that started it a ref; an agent's live state and each proc's numbers recorded as facts on its entity when they change, by their owner (root records an agent's status when its view changes; the writer its own numbers when it commits), never polled, no write on an idle system. Deletes root's view as a second source of what an agent is."
