  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-test/an-agents-links-pull-from-its-root seon.task-test/an-agents-live-state-is-facts-on-its-entity seon.procs-test/root-records-what-it-observes-of-its-agents seon.agent-test/a-resumed-branchs-agent-is-this-boots}}
 {:seon.task/name "walk"
  :seon.task/plan "The one mechanism every view of Seon's data goes through: the owner's window, root's page and the model's context (owner, 2026-10-23: \"locations should be based on a starting EID and we pull from there to render the data what one hop away\"; \"I want logical solution for everything so we aren't inventing new shit\"; \"rendered with render functions not manual wiring\"). What v1 did (~/src/seon/resources/seon/schemas/seon.agent.edn :agent, render/walk.clj neighborhood at :seon.render/distance 1): an entity's schema declares the neighbours a view of it shows as :seon.render/units, attributes in pull's own syntax (a leading _ the reverse ref), components included; the walk pulls the entity and those attributes, and each neighbour renders by its own schema's render. Bring the rule, not v1's 1263 lines (most are SCI caps and caches). Build: (1) :seon.render/units on the schemas this needs: the agent's (:seon.db/agent-fact: its task, its evaluations :seon.eval/_agent, its model calls :seon.model-call/_agent, the messages it took :seon.message/_agent, its root), the task's (its plan, proofs), root's (:seon.root/root-fact: its agents :seon.agent/_root, the open problems); (2) seon.render/walk: one d/pull of an eid with its own attributes and its units (on a branch: the branch's db, or seon.union's U for what spans branches, ids issued once store-wide, docs/storage.md#ids), plus what its string attributes cite (seon.ref/parse, each citation pulled at the depth its delimiter asks, as seon.context pulls it), answering the tree as one map (owner, 2026-10-23: \"We are returning a giant map right? so we can show what rendered it, what it is (a doc a symbol to a file, etc ?)\"): each node its value and what it is (an entity and its schema, a var, a doc section path#anchor, a file path:line, a citation found in a string), where it came from (eid and attribute, or the string and citation that pulled it) and the render that showed it with its :as, so an agent reading it knows what to change: the producer, the render or the citation. Every key in that map is fully namespaced and has a registered schema (owner, 2026-10-23: \"we only use fully namespaced schemas and we don't want to be cute with :you stuff\"): a pulled entity keeps its own attributes, and the walk's own parts are schemas of seon.render (its references, each citation's place, which render made which part). A citation's content is pulled once per context and cited by its number where it appears, so two citations of one var never repeat it; What each kind of value the walk pulls costs and who needs it (owner via root, 2026-10-23: \"I want each piece of data we are processing to be looked at and determined if we should just be dumping the whole thing in without regard to whether it's relevant\"), measured as the model's text on one run (the /walk root, task seon.render/config-shows-where-each-value-came-from), before -> after: root's config (its 108 :seon.config/* attributes, a 14 KB value) 1,801 -> 0 B: nobody needs it whole, the model needs the keys its task's code reads; root's focused render (seon.render.ai/root-fact) shows its boot, generation and how it last stopped, and a config key reaches a context only as one the task's code reads (to build); root 87 B, one line; an agent 386 -> 94 B: status, pid, branch and the form running (seon.render.ai/agent-fact), its description only for an agent no run started, cut at the producer (seon.root/observed: a run's agent's :task was its task id, which pulled the whole task into every walk of the agent a second time, 375 B -> 0); a run 753 -> 159 B: id, status or outcome and reason, model, turn of max, branch (seon.render.ai/run-fact); its plan is its task's and its boot root's, both cut; a task 906-1,265 B, its plan 263 B and proofs 53 B: shown whole, the model's work is in them; a cited var at its citation's depth 605 B: whole, the depth chooses it. Totals: an agent's walk 3,920 -> 945 B; root's 2,594 -> 703 B; a run's 2,589 -> 2,042 B. Not on this run, to measure when seon.context/evaluations records them: turns, evaluations, messages, problems, doc sections, schema forms. Calls for the owner: whether config shows the keys the task's code reads (a reach over the :seon.config/* keywords its forms name) or only a key a text cites; whether a task as a run's neighbour is whole or its plan and proofs only; root's walk shows every boot's agents (two for one run after a restart): this boot's only, or ended ones collapsed into one line. prose is markdown and facts are data (owner, 2026-10-23: \"Somethings are better as data and some are better as markdown\"): a value whose schema is prose (help, a plan, a doc section, a message, a reply) renders as markdown and is parsed for its citations; an entity, proofs, cost, the references and the provenance render as data an agent can read back and use; each value's render decides by its schema, so one evaluation may hold both; (3) the tree printed by the one printer (seon.print with seon.render/nested): the root of the walk by its :html render :as :block, each unit :as :tile; a page part is drawn by its :html render alone, never its :ai face (task a-page-part-renders-only-its-view). What is not useful is cut at its producer or given a focused render on its schema, never filtered by a view (owner: \"if shit isn't useful we either cut it at the producer or make specific renders to help focus the context or html\"). Read first: docs/render.md, seon.render (render-of, nested, print-ai), seon.print, seon.ref, seon.context's pulls (what it pulls today and at which depth), v1's walk. Replaces nothing yet: page and evaluations delete the hand-built producers."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render/the-agents-links-are-refs"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/the-html-fallback-folds-only-what-nests seon.task-test/a-new-boot-leaves-the-earlier-boots-agents}}
 {:seon.task/name "page-walks-only-the-rows-shown"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a page renders only its first members (`seon.render.ui/page`, `:seon.config.render.list/history`), but every pass walks every member first: `seon.render/page` walks the entity on main and on its branch, each unit whole (`seon.render/walk` pulls every evaluation and its record), measured at 5 contracted calls and about 41 KB an evaluation a pass (`seon.render-cost-test/a-page-walks-each-db-once`: 41 MB at 1,000), and root's page shows every agent of its boot as a tile, unpaged. Correct: a unit shown as a list is pulled newest first only as far as the members shown (pull's :limit on the reverse ref, or an index read of the newest), its count read apart, so a pass costs the members on screen, not the list; root's tiles paged like the list. Measure first: the transactions of the newest members by index, against the pull."
