 {:seon.task/name "a-ctx-is-shown-as-its-parts"
  :seon.task/plan "Slice 2a of the one derivation (docs/render.md, revision 3, root's approval of 2026-10-26): the pure functions the page and the model are drawn from. (seon.render/render-ctx ctx) answers an agent's ctx as ordered keyed parts [{:key k :value v}]: [:task] (the task's relevant keys), [:proofs] (each proof's status and first failure, from the newest turn's observed results), [:run] (the newest run's id, status, turn, max turns, outcome and reason), then the conversation: [:eval id] per eval and [:message id] per message, in the order they were written. It is pure in ctx: equal ctx, equal parts; it reads no db and no config. Noise stays out of the parts: no :db/id, no :t or :commit, no :seon.agent/answered-t, no turn basis but the newest's observed results (the owner: every change is told, so a change without meaning is a data defect). (seon.render/faces part) answers {:html :text}, both default faces from one selection of the one printer (seon.print.select/show with :outputs #{:text :hiccup}, where render/html and render/ai select twice today, render.clj:113-132), a schema's :seon.render/html or :seon.render/ai overriding its face alone. (seon.render/drawn memo part f) memoises a face per part key, a hit when the fn the var holds now and the value are both identical? (a redefined fn draws again). Replaces nothing yet: 2b moves the agent page onto these parts and deletes what they replace."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-view-is-its-ctxs-parts seon.render-test/a-parts-faces-are-one-selection seon.render-test/a-part-draws-once-per-value}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-page-is-its-components"
  :seon.task/plan "Render build 3 (docs/render.md, its refinement: a server-side ratom with the cursor inferred, and Datastar; the owner's go, 2026-10-26). What exists: an agent's page is its parts (seon.render/render-ctx as a vector of keyed parts, each drawn by part-faces into html through the :drawn memo, compared by hand-built keys: context/renders' task-key, cited-key, related-key, first-t), sent to root (seon.repl/parts), laid out by seon.render.ui/parts-page and diffed by html per unit in the render proc (step-units). The lab (shared notes render-investigation-2026-10-26.md, its ratom addenda) measured the replacement. Build: (1) seon.ratom, reagent.ratom ported with its names (RAtom, Reaction, run-in-reaction, flush!, dispose!) and a tracking view that infers the cursor, its hit test over seon.ctx/changes, the one diff; (2) the walk, in the agent where ctx lives: a component is a call [f opts & children] keyed by f and its children, its root element's id its key; a render whose opts declare :seon.ctx/ctx reads ctx through the view, one declaring :seon.db/db reads the db under d/with-reads; one cache (the component's reaction, args, db reads and output); a ledger of calls, hits, misses and allocation by the render's var; the view unwrapped where a value is drawn; (seon.render/page ctx) and (seon.render/page-diff p0 p1), [[op key hiccup]] with op :replace, :append (a keyed child new under a parent whose own output is otherwise unchanged) or :remove; (3) the agent page's layout as one component over ctx, its lists keyed by eid, the eval's handle anchor drawn by the eval's render, and the task render drawing from ctx what the task needs (each cited var's source, its deftests with source and last result, each cited section's text, the whole plan: the scratch paper context-for-20-turns-2026-10-26.md (git history) D1-D4) under cuts naming the form for the rest; (4) root's render proc sends an agent's frame (its page-diff, or its whole page for a keyframe) as Datastar events (seon.web/chunk-of), and draws an agent with no process by the same walk over its branch. Deletes render-ctx's parts, part-faces, part-html, drawn, context/renders' hand keys, seon.repl's :drawn memo, ui/parts-page and ui/agent-part, and step-units' compares for an agent page. Root's page and the debug page stay on the unit walk (every-page-is-its-parts). Follow-up, owned here: a plan edit costs 22.6M ticks on a3 (seon.reconcile/root-work-is-tasks), more than the worst change today, because task-data runs again whole and seon.doc/cited-view of its 8 cited sections is 4.6M of its 5.6M (about 580k a section; parsing the plan 0.46M, similar 0.17M, printing 0.58M); once ctx holds each cited section's text (ctx-atom's D3, its task pattern's :sections), task-data reads them from ctx and cited-view leaves the render. Owner: agent view."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ratom-test/basic-ratom seon.ratom-test/test-unsubscribe seon.ratom-test/test-dispose seon.ratom-test/catching seon.ratom-test/inferred-paths seon.ratom-test/a-view-is-a-map
                      seon.render-test/a-component-re-runs-on-what-it-read seon.render-test/a-new-member-is-an-append seon.render-test/a-handle-links-to-its-eval seon.render-test/the-task-draws-what-it-needs
                      seon.render-cost-test/a-change-costs-its-components}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-status-before-any-model-turn"
  :seon.task/plan "Found building revision 4.2 (jolt/model-reads-the-page): the page's [:status] part (seon.render.ui/status over seon.context/turn-state) is drawn from the newest model turn's recorded basis (seon.render/render-ctx picks the run's :model turns), so an agent whose run has only an outside read (seon.turn/context-block, origin :outside), or none yet, shows no status: the person and an outside agent read no proofs, no done rule's reasons, no test run. What the outside read observed is recorded too (its model call's :seon.model-call/basis :observed), so the fix is in render-ctx: draw [:status] from the newest turn of either origin, its turn count only for a model turn (turn-input already leaves it out for :outside). Before any turn at all, the part is absent, which is true: nothing observed yet. Proof: an agent page whose run has one outside read shows its proofs in [:status]."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/the-status-shows-what-an-outside-read-observed}}
 {:seon.task/name "stale-sections-are-a-part-of-the-page"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner (2026-10-26): :seon.doc/read as an error's subject is a code smell, and so is the machinery behind it; the database knows exactly what changed, so only changed parts render again and the rest hit the render cache; test it with agents in the REPL, then propose the design, and do not build yet another system. What exists (checked 2026-10-27): `seon.problems/stale-sections` compares form ids recorded at load (:seon.doc/read, `seon.doc/read-map`) with current ones and reads history for meaning (`seon.program/meaning`: :var/doc, :var/contract); a section loaded with no read asks git (`seon.root/wiki-baseline`, the :history effect `seon.fx/wiki-history`, `seon.doc/baseline-plan`, `baseline-reads`, `baseline-failed`; :seon.doc/baseline-unread). Measured in a lab root (412 sections, 2,990 vars, 1,155 citations): 0 sections held a :read, because boot and each watcher batch load docs before source (`my.root/load-kinds!`), so the read is taken against a program that is not there yet. Experiments, in a Seon agent's REPL on a lab store branch: (1) the rule 'a section is stale when a cited var has a datom on its meaning in a transaction after the section's text or review' against today's scan after random edits of a docstring, a contract and a body (the results are exactly the sections citing a var whose meaning changed after them; a body edit makes none); (2) its cost in contracted calls and bytes at 412 sections and at x10 (one eavt seek per citation against today's scan); (3) the same as a part of the page on the one path (view, page, diffs) whose input is ctx values, so an unchanged var is identical? and the part's memo a pointer test; (4) source loaded before docs at boot and per batch so a section written with its code is current. If they bear out, it deletes :seon.doc/read, :seon.doc/baseline-unread, wiki-baseline, baseline-plan, baseline-reads, baseline-failed, read-map, the :history effect, the form-id and history comparison in stale-sections and the form ids of :seon.doc/reviewed; given up: drift before the store existed and Reviewed lines in old commit messages. Related: seon.problems/a-section-goes-stale-when-what-it-cites-changes-meaning (the rule, already in stale-sections). Proof to write: the property in (1) and a cost proof in (2)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-value-is-rendered-by-the-newest-render-of-its-schema"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-29: \"automatic per eval result auto rendering and to override by just writing a function that renders that. A render function is just a function that takes in the data we need to render and optional args we can auto inject like the current db or even the current ctx if they define the inputs and returns hiccup (seon.render/html) ... just a query which gets the default and any other render functions and sorts them by newest first\"; \"if we don't know what something is we return a warning/error so the agents are encouraged to fix it and we only auto render things we know what they are and have specified render functions\".

What exists: seon.render/render-of selects a render by the value's own :seon.render/html, then the one its schema declares (:seon.render/html on the schema entry, seon.render/declared), then the printer; seon.render/reads fills :seon.ctx/ctx and :seon.db/db when a render's opts contract names them; the program db holds each var's contract (:var/contract). A schema's declared render is global: an agent re-pointing it changes everyone's view at merge.

Correct:
1. A render is any function whose contract is [:=> [:cat opts K] :seon.render/html] for a named schema K; its opts contract names what it is injected (:seon.ctx/ctx, :seon.db/db).
2. A value's renders are one query over the db's :var/contract: the functions taking K, newest first by the transaction of their contract; the first is used. The schema's declared render is the oldest candidate, the default. The answer is a cache keyed by the db's t (AGENTS 5).
3. What a value is: a ctx key is its schema; an entity its identity attribute's schema; an eval's value the named return schema of its outermost call (after macroexpansion) when that is a program var.
4. Unknown: printed by the one printer, never guessed (no validation against candidates). When the outermost call is a program var whose return schema is generic (:any, :map), the answer carries a warning naming the var and the fix (name its return schema); code that is not the program's (clojure.core, interop, a literal) is printed with no warning, since there is nothing in the program to fix.
5. Each eval whose value has a render is drawn as a tile beside the conversation (render-ctx's p-tiles today).

Replaces: the schema-declared render as the only way to choose one. Scoping: a render the agent defines wins on its branch at once; merged, it is the newest on main."
  :seon.task/kind :design
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-render-tag-is-a-checked-schema"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by AGENTS section 3 step 4 its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: a value's render schema is named in metadata (:seon.render/schema) or guessed from its keys (seon.render/schema-of), never validated, and many tag schemas are bare [:map] (seon.render.edn: :seon.agent/state, :seon.context/run, :seon.context/status, :seon.test/proved, :seon.render/render-ctx, root-ctx, root-input, :my.agent/lines, :seon.context/namespaces). Metadata does not survive the store, so the record grew a second channel (:schema; 952fe4f3a0, 21e364cabc, 710b32c164), and new renders hung on with-meta (0a58e8aa31, 51825107d1). The change: one constructor seon.render/as [k v], the only writer of :seon.render/schema, validates v against k while contracts are on; each tag schema gets its real keys; if the record's tag then lives in the value's declared shape, the record's :schema channel goes, and error-key sniffing goes (an error's :seon.error/type is its schema). Sweep the 32 sites of the key in src. Docs: docs/render.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-tagged-value-is-checked-against-its-tag}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-way-to-make-what-an-agent-is-sent"
  :seon.task/plan "What is wrong: what an agent is sent is not the render of its record. The owner, 2026-11-04: \"pull pattern <-> synch @ctx atom -> hiccup render -> model context\"; \"the different bits of data are only calculated once for everything\"; \"okay go for it\". The walk draws the page, and seon.context/request assembles the transcript beside it: it queries the db for records @ctx holds, prints each eval a second time, and has its own diff (seon.context/part-change), hashes and cold draws per earlier turn (50-178 MB a commit on a real page). What should be true: pull pattern -> @ctx -> the one walk -> seon.render/model, one read of the walk, one lookup of a model render, one order and filter of the conversation, nothing new but what html to pixels needs. Steps, each its own merge, reporting lines deleted against added: 0, record the bytes of three sessions (seon.context-test/the-recorded-requests-come-out-the-same), which steps to 5 reproduce. 1, one read of the walk (seon.repl/page-parts and page-hiccup, seon.render/placed-hiccup and placed-data). 2, the bugs as classes: seon.repl/record-details drops :recorded from what the model reads; seon.repl/the-walk fixes the layout once; a woken agent is sent its own work! eval, inferred, a test confirming it first (three filters of the conversation disagree). 3, the unwatched walk draws #p-task alone (:only), lifted when a viewer arrives. 4, seon.render/model, replacing model-of and model-printed, and records declaring their model renders. 5, records read from @ctx: seon.context/records and seon.render.ui/conversation one function, the 2026-10-26 ruling. 6, the owner's D1: the page is only ever the last message, whole and latest, and part-change, :hashes, was, :live and parts-message are deleted; a real run's cache hits to the owner before step 7. 7, D2: task, status, run and errors stay data, cites, namespaces and searches become structure, [render-fn args] read from the walk's :out; measured against f19afd9d35, six tasks, two runs each. 8, D3-D7: the browser's memory and time measured first, then the image. 9, seon.context/task-data, cited, seon.render.ui/proofs. No bound is raised without the owner; one that improves is re-pinned, old and new numbers in the merge's report. Carried from the superseded seon.context/a-changed-part-sends-its-change (approved by the owner, 2026-10-30, docs/decisions.md): measured on md-scan #28's last request (176k estimated, 146k by the provider): page parts 72k (41%: 40 parts messages; the cited code and docs parts re-sent whole at turns 5 and 20, 13.6k and 9.9k, all five though one changed; status 11 times at 3-5k), evals 67k (one (my.agent/prove) answer 16k, mostly :why of 182 deferred process tests), replies 35k, system 2.3k. Steps, each measured on #28's record before and after: (1) a part that answers data sends clojure.data/diff of its value against the value last sent (regenerable from the db at that commit, so the agent may hold it as a cache keyed by part and commit); (2) cited vars at preview depth (signature, docstring), the source on request, and only the cited part that changed redrawn; (3) the turn counter (n of max-turns, tokens) on each eval's one-line outcome, not the turn line part; (4) the prove answer's :why drawn as a count with its handle. Then, if stale parts still weigh, the one whole redraw (the 2026-10-29 ruling), triggered by measured stale tokens (a config key: the owner's go). A defect beside it: seon.context/parts-of rebuilds nothing for #28's turns 6-19, so the debug page cannot show those requests whole."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-recorded-requests-come-out-the-same seon.context-test/a-run-printed-once-sends-what-a-cold-read-sends}}
 {:seon.task/name "roots-walk-of-a-branch-draws-the-branchs-layout"
  :seon.task/plan "What is wrong: root's walk of an agent's branch (seon.render.proc/branch-walk!, and its cold draws) reads seon.render/declared-var under root's registry, not the branch's: seon.render.proc binds none, so a layout the agent registered on its branch draws on its own walk (seon.repl/the-walk, what its model reads) and not on the page root serves for an ended agent. Found by the sweep of step 2 of seon.render/one-way-to-make-what-an-agent-is-sent, whose fix for a walk's held layout has no reach here. What should be true: docs/ctx.md's page is the agent's to change holds for every reader of its page, live or ended; seon.repl/branch-registry reads a branch's schema changes, and the same read serves root's walk. Proof: an ended agent's page, drawn by root, shows the layout its branch registered."
